derivations.claim:
[Directional component is a single spin-$s$ block] Fix a unit $n$ and choose an oriented orthonormal frame $(e^{(1)},e^{(2)},e^{(3)})$ of $\mathbb R^{3}$ with $e^{(1)}=n$; put $A_{i}:=\sum_{l}e^{(i)}_{l}J_{l}$ for $i=1,2,3$; expanding the commutator with [F1] gives $[A_{i},A_{j}]=\sum_{l,m}e^{(i)}_{l}e^{(j)}_{m}[J_{l},J_{m}]=i\sum_{k} igl(\sum_{l,m}\varepsilon_{lmk}e^{(i)}_{l}e^{(j)}_{m} igr)J_{k}=i\sum_{k}(e^{(i)}	imes e^{(j)})_{k}J_{k}$, which equals $iA_{i'}$ with $i'$ the index for which $e^{(i)}	imes e^{(j)}=e^{(i')}$, and $\sum_{i}A_{i}^{2}=\sum_{k}J_{k}^{2}=s(s+1)I$ by the orthonormality of the frame; by [F2] applied to the triple $(A_{1},A_{2},A_{3})$ there is a decomposition into blocks with parameters $j_{
u}$ satisfying $j_{
u}(j_{
u}+1)=s(s+1)$ on each block, so $j_{
u}=s$ and every block has dimension $2s+1=\dim V_{s}$; hence there is exactly one block and $A_{1}=n\cdot J$ has the simple eigenvalues $s,s-1,\dots,-s$, which is the spectrum statement of (a) at the matrix level.

